  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe slow moving upper trough responsible for rain over past few\ndays will become increasing sheared in nature into this evening\nand will become more progressive as it shifts to the eastern \nGreat Lakes by Monday morning. Weakening nature of this trough \nhas left an elongated mid level deformation zone across far NE \nIN/NW OH this afternoon which was a focal point for some locally\nheavy rainfall earlier this morning. Better deep column \nmoisture axis is beginning to exit this area this afternoon and \nexpecting these deformation-forced showers to continue to sag \nsoutheast and exit the area early this evening. The potential \nfor flooding has diminished and the Flood Watch for NE IN/NW OH \nhas been cancelled.\n\nAn upper speed max diving across the Great Lakes region may aid in \nthe development of a few isolated showers this afternoon, \nparticularly along what appears to be a weak differential \nheating boundary across Lower Michigan downstream of this \ntrough. With lessening deep layer moisture these showers are not\nexpected to pose any significant hydro threat before \ndiminishing early this evening.\n\nAn overall quiet pattern will then take shape tonight through \nWednesday. A longwave upper ridge building across the Mid/Upper MS \nValley will allow an expansive low level anticyclone to overspread \nthe Great Lakes on Monday. This should provide slightly below normal \ntemperatures for Monday with some slight modification to near\nnormal temps for Tuesday.\n\nGuidance has been fairly consistent in a stronger warm/moist \nlow level advection push late Wednesday night into Thursday \ndownstream of broad eastern Pacific wave. It appears as though \nlocal area may be located on southern periphery of stronger deep\nlayer shear profiles, and possible late tonight/Thursday \nmorning timing leads to low confidence in any isolated \nstrong/severe threat. Likely PoPs have been maintained on \nThursday, but still a good deal of uncertainty exists regarding \ninstability magnitudes during the day Thursday depending on \nevolution of upstream convection late Wed night/Thu AM.\n\nMuch of the long term period will favor a wavering baroclinic zone \nwith periodic chances of showers and storms as the potential exists \nfor several progressive waves to affect area in west-northwest upper \nflow into next weekend. Predictability is on the low side with \n12- hour PoPs in this pattern, but medium range guidance does \nseem to suggest that longwave ridging may amplify across central\nCONUS for early next week with a transition back to above \nnormal temperatures.",
